    {
      "id": "p0076-b006",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "During the 1990s and earlier, hardware limitations made it unwieldy to support the thousands of kanji characters. This led developers to rely more heavily on hiragana and katakana, with limited kanji support when possible. All three writing systems are commonly supported in modern games, which in turn gives the \"mostly hiragana and katakana\" writing style a strong retro vibe.",
        "raw": "During the 1990s and earlier, hardware limitations made it unwieldy to support\nthe thousands of kanji characters. This led developers to rely more heavily on\nhiragana and katakana, with limited kanji support when possible. All three\nwriting systems are commonly supported in modern games, which in turn\ngives the \"mostly hiragana and katakana\" writing style a strong retro vibe.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29366265,
          0.35526429,
          0.9032944,
          0.4391
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29004484,
          0.35455,
          0.90532688,
          0.44017143
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          14,
          15,
          16,
          17,
          18
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "20世纪90年代及更早，硬件限制让游戏难以容纳数千个汉字。开发者因此更多依赖平假名和片假名，条件允许时才加入少量汉字。现代游戏普遍支持三套文字，也因此让“主要使用平假名与片假名”的写法带上了浓厚的复古气息。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
